      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright © 2012 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
            "content": "GIP is a peptide hormone of therapeutic interest in type 2 diabetes and obesity. This study evaluated pGIP/neo STC-1 as a potential K-cell model for studying GIP secretion."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "We evaluated cellular storage and medium accumulation of GIP along with other gastrointestinal peptides cholecystokinin (CCK), peptide YY (PYY), obestatin and ghrelin over 72 h and probed possible intracellular signals (PKA, PKC, Ca(2+) and GPCR) involved in peptide hormone synthesis/secretion."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "Results demonstrate for the first time that pGIP/Neo STC-1 cells produce and secrete 3 to 6 times more GIP than STC-1. The cells clearly retain the ability to synthesize and secrete CCK and PYY but reduced levels indicate a shift towards a predominantly K-cell phenotype. Furthermore, gastric peptides such as obestatin and ghrelin are not produced in either STC-1 or pGIP/Neo STC-1 cells."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "DISCUSSION",
            "content": "This study demonstrates the potential usefulness of pGIP/Neo cells for studying GIP secretion and further investigations will establish its suitability for investigating hormone release in vitro."
          }
        ]
      },
